R2. new otherwise returns an empty cache with the supplied entry-count capacity and period, and pending = 0. Capacity zero is valid.
R3. Keys and values accept their full u64 and i64 ranges. Each key occupies exactly one slot regardless of value.
R4. Every resident entry has a frequency in 1..=7. A newly inserted key starts at 1.
R5. Global recency is a strict oldest-to-newest order of keys based on insertion and successful hit/update call order. No wall clock is used.
R6. Eviction priority is lowest frequency first, with oldest global recency breaking frequency ties. Numeric key order never breaks a recency tie.
R7. snapshot returns cloned entries in eviction priority order (frequency ascending, then oldest recency); it does not mutate any state.
R8. capacity and pending return stored values and do not count as operations.
R9. get, remove, and a put with nonzero capacity each count as ONE operation, including get/remove misses and updates of an existing key.
R10. Immediately BEFORE the counted operation acts, increment pending. When this would make pending equal period, automatically age every currently resident entry by one round and reset pending to 0.
R11. One aging round maps f to ceil(f / 2), equivalently (f + 1) / 2. Frequency 1 stays 1; 3 becomes 2; 7 becomes 4.
R12. Automatic aging occurs before lookup, frequency credit, value replacement or eviction for that operation. A new key inserted at a boundary is not aged in that boundary round.
R13. A get hit returns its value, adds one frequency credit saturating at 7, and moves the key to newest recency.
R14. A get miss returns None; it still counts and may age entries, but leaves surviving recency unchanged.
R15. put on a resident key replaces its value, adds one frequency credit saturating at 7, and touches it to newest. It returns an empty eviction list and never removes another key.
R16. put on a missing key evicts one priority victim if full, then stores the new key with frequency 1 and newest recency. It returns either [] or [victim].
R17. put when capacity is zero returns Disabled BEFORE counting, aging, or any mutation. Failed puts leave pending and all other state unchanged.
R18. remove returns the removed value or None. Removal does not credit or touch any survivor, but its boundary aging still occurs before deletion.
R19. age(rounds) explicitly runs exactly rounds aging rounds on all current residents. rounds = 0 is a complete no-op; rounds up to 255 must work.
R20. age never counts as an operation and never changes pending, capacity or recency.
R21. resize installs the capacity and evicts entries in priority order until length <= capacity. Return all victims in that order, with frequency/recency recomputed from surviving entries.
R22. resize never counts as an operation, never ages entries, and preserves pending even if it removes all entries.
R23. resize(0) empties the cache; later put fails until capacity is increased. A get or remove on a disabled cache still counts as an operation.
R24. Growing or same-size resize keeps entries, frequencies and recency unchanged and returns [].
R25. Aging merges frequency buckets without changing recency. The tie-break after merging uses original global recency, not prior frequency or key order.
R26. Frequency saturation must still touch the key: hits and updates at frequency 7 affect future ties.
R27. period = 1 ages before every counted operation. Even operations on an empty cache obey pending logic. period = u32::MAX must not overflow pending arithmetic.
R28. Deleting and reinserting a key resets its frequency to 1 and recency to newest; no old frequency history survives.
